WEBVTT

00:12:58.116 --> 00:13:00.556
who I initially thought
I had nothing in common with

00:13:00.556 --> 00:13:04.196
and through conversations,
through breaking bread,

00:13:04.236 --> 00:13:06.836
through finding other ways
to interact with each other

00:13:06.836 --> 00:13:08.036
we found commonality,

00:13:08.036 --> 00:13:10.956
and they really have advocated
for me in huge ways.

00:13:10.996 --> 00:13:14.076
WPR: The book, it seems like
in your writing of it,

00:13:14.116 --> 00:13:16.316
women of color were really
at the center of it,

00:13:16.316 --> 00:13:19.676
were really a big part
of how you framed this book.

00:13:19.716 --> 00:13:24.196
But I'm interested to hear sort of what
the response has been from allies

00:13:24.236 --> 00:13:27.396
and people who are now part
of that community.

00:13:27.396 --> 00:13:30.636
DP: I think a lot of these concepts apply
outside of women of color.